DEEP = [
T("What self really is",
  "When you write `rex.bark()`, Python actually runs `Dog.bark(rex)`: it looks up `bark` on the class and passes the object in as the first argument. That argument is `self`. Forget the `self` parameter and Python complains that the method got one argument too many.",
  "class Dog:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def bark(self):\n        print(self.name, 'says woof')\n\nrex = Dog('Rex')\nrex.bark()        # Rex says woof\nDog.bark(rex)     # exactly the same call",
  "Methods are plain functions stored on the class. The dot syntax `object.method()` is a shortcut that fills in the first argument with the object.",
  "Understanding this explains most method errors, like \"takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given\" when `self` is missing."),
Q("`rex` is a Dog. What does `Dog.bark(rex)` do?", ["The same as `rex.bark()`", "It raises an error: methods can't be called on the class", "It creates a new Dog"], "The same as `rex.bark()`",
  "`rex.bark()` is shorthand for `Dog.bark(rex)`: the object is passed in as `self`."),
T("Methods that return values",
  "A method that prints can only show a result. A method that **returns** gives the result back, so the caller can store it, compare it or print it. Most methods that compute something should return.",
  "class Circle:\n    def __init__(self, radius):\n        self.radius = radius\n\n    def area(self):\n        return 3.14 * self.radius ** 2\n\nc = Circle(2)\nprint(c.area())          # 12.56\nprint(c.area() > 10)     # True: the value can be used",
  "Same as with functions: `return` ends the method and hands a value to whoever called it. `self` gives the method the object's data to compute from.",
  "Calculations on an object's data: totals, areas, ages, scores. Keep printing for the code that uses the object."),
C("Disk usage",
  "Add a `percent_used` method to Disk that **returns** used divided by total, times 100. The last line checks it against an alert threshold and prints: 80.0 True",
  "class Disk:\n    def __init__(self, total_gb, used_gb):\n        self.total_gb = total_gb\n        self.used_gb = used_gb\n\n    # TODO: add percent_used(self)\n\nd = Disk(500, 400)\nprint(d.percent_used(), d.percent_used() > 75)",
  "class Disk:\n    def __init__(self, total_gb, used_gb):\n        self.total_gb = total_gb\n        self.used_gb = used_gb\n\n    def percent_used(self):\n        return self.used_gb / self.total_gb * 100\n\nd = Disk(500, 400)\nprint(d.percent_used(), d.percent_used() > 75)",
  "def percent_used(self):\n    return self.used_gb / self.total_gb * 100",
  [("Read the object's own data", "Inside the method, the sizes are `self.used_gb` and `self.total_gb`. `self` is the disk the method was called on.",
    "class Box:\n    def __init__(self, size):\n        self.size = size\n\n    def double(self):\n        return self.size * 2\n\nprint(Box(5).double())"),
   ("Return, don't print", "The last line uses the result twice, once in a comparison. If the method printed instead, the comparison would get None and crash.",
    "def half(n):\n    return n / 2\n\nprint(half(8) + 1)  # 5.0")]),
T("Default values in __init__",
  "`__init__` takes parameters like any function, so they can have defaults. Callers then only pass what differs from the usual.",
  "class Player:\n    def __init__(self, name, level=1):\n        self.name = name\n        self.level = level\n\nnew = Player('Ada')\npro = Player('Bob', 30)\nprint(new.level, pro.level)   # 1 30",
  "Default values are filled in when an argument is left out, exactly as in plain functions.",
  "Settings with a common value: a starting score, a default color, an empty list of items (created inside __init__)."),
C("Default ticket priority",
  "Most help desk tickets are routine. Give `priority` a default of 'low' in Ticket's `__init__`. The prints should show low then high.",
  "class Ticket:\n    def __init__(self, title, priority):\n        # TODO: make priority default to 'low'\n        self.title = title\n        self.priority = priority\n\nprint(Ticket('Printer jam').priority)\nprint(Ticket('Server down', 'high').priority)",
  "class Ticket:\n    def __init__(self, title, priority='low'):\n        self.title = title\n        self.priority = priority\n\nprint(Ticket('Printer jam').priority)\nprint(Ticket('Server down', 'high').priority)",
  "def __init__(self, title, priority='low'):",
  [("Defaults go in the parameter list", "Write `priority='low'` in the `def __init__(...)` line. Then `Ticket('Printer jam')` works without a second argument.",
    "def greet(name, punctuation='!'):\n    return 'Hi ' + name + punctuation\n\nprint(greet('Ada'), greet('Bob', '?'))"),
   ("Passing a value still wins", "`Ticket('Server down', 'high')` passes 'high', which replaces the default. Only change the def line; the body stays the same.",
    "def area(w, h=1):\n    return w * h\n\nprint(area(3), area(3, 2))  # 3 6")]),
T("Class attributes vs instance attributes",
  "An attribute set on `self` belongs to one object. A variable written directly in the class body is a **class attribute**, shared by every object of that class. When you read `obj.x`, Python checks the object first, then the class.",
  "class Dog:\n    species = 'canine'          # class attribute: shared\n\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name        # instance attribute: per dog\n\na = Dog('Rex')\nb = Dog('Fido')\nprint(a.species, b.species)   # canine canine\nprint(a.name, b.name)         # Rex Fido",
  "Each object keeps its own attributes in a small dictionary. If a name isn't found there, the lookup falls back to the class's dictionary.",
  "Constants that every object shares, like a species name, a tax rate or a count of objects created."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["wolf canine", "wolf wolf", "canine canine"], "wolf canine",
  "`a.species = 'wolf'` creates an instance attribute on `a` only. `b` has no attribute of its own, so it still finds the class's 'canine'.",
  code="class Dog:\n    species = 'canine'\n\na = Dog()\nb = Dog()\na.species = 'wolf'\nprint(a.species, b.species)",
  feedback={"wolf wolf": "Assigning through `a` creates an attribute on `a` alone. The class attribute, which `b` still reads, doesn't change."}),
T("The shared-list trap",
  "A list written as a class attribute is one list shared by all objects. Appending to it through one object changes it for all of them. Mutable data that each object should own belongs in `__init__`, on `self`.",
  "class Team:\n    members = []                # shared by every Team!\n\nred = Team()\nblue = Team()\nred.members.append('Ada')\nprint(blue.members)             # ['Ada']: surprise\n\nclass FixedTeam:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.members = []       # a new list per team",
  "`red.members.append` doesn't assign anything: it finds `members` on the class and changes that one list in place.",
  "Any per-object collection: a cart's items, a player's inventory, a team's members. It's a favorite interview trap, alongside the mutable default argument."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["['sit']", "[]", "It raises AttributeError"], "['sit']",
  "`tricks` is a class attribute, so both dogs share one list. Rex's append shows up for Fido.",
  code="class Dog:\n    tricks = []\n\nrex = Dog()\nfido = Dog()\nrex.tricks.append('sit')\nprint(fido.tricks)"),
C("Fix the shared port list",
  "An audit shows the database server with port 443 open, but only the web server opened it: both servers share one list. Move `open_ports` into an `__init__` as `self.open_ports = []`, so it prints [443] then []",
  "class Server:\n    open_ports = []\n\n    def open_port(self, port):\n        self.open_ports.append(port)\n\nweb = Server()\ndb = Server()\nweb.open_port(443)\nprint(web.open_ports)\nprint(db.open_ports)",
  "class Server:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.open_ports = []\n\n    def open_port(self, port):\n        self.open_ports.append(port)\n\nweb = Server()\ndb = Server()\nweb.open_port(443)\nprint(web.open_ports)\nprint(db.open_ports)",
  "def __init__(self):\n    self.open_ports = []",
  [("Where the list is created matters", "A list in the class body is made once, when the class is defined. A list in `__init__` is made again for every new object.",
    "class Bag:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []\n\na, b = Bag(), Bag()\na.items.append(1)\nprint(a.items, b.items)  # [1] []"),
   ("Replace, don't add", "Delete the `open_ports = []` line and put an `__init__(self)` method in its place that sets `self.open_ports = []`. open_port can stay as it is.",
    "class Shelf:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.books = []\n\nprint(Shelf().books)")]),
T("Objects are shared by reference",
  "A variable holds a reference to an object, not a copy. `b = a` makes a second name for the same object, so a change through `b` shows through `a`. `a is b` checks for the same object; `==` checks for equal values (which, for your own classes, means the same object unless you add `__eq__`).",
  "class Box:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []\n\na = Box()\nb = a                # same box, two names\nb.items.append('pen')\nprint(a.items)       # ['pen']\nprint(a is b)        # True\nprint(Box() is Box())   # False: two different boxes",
  "Assignment never copies an object. It points another name at the existing one, like two labels on the same jar.",
  "Passing objects to functions (the function can change them), storing the same object in two lists, and spotting aliasing bugs."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["1", "0", "It raises an error"], "1",
  "`b = a` doesn't copy the box. Both names point at one object, so its list has one item.",
  code="class Box:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []\n\na = Box()\nb = a\nb.items.append(1)\nprint(len(a.items))"),
T("Protecting an object's data",
  "Check arguments in `__init__` and raise an error for bad values, so an invalid object can never exist. A leading underscore, like `self._balance`, is a convention meaning \"internal: use the methods instead\". Python doesn't block access; it's a signal to other programmers.",
  "class Account:\n    def __init__(self, balance):\n        if balance < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"balance can't be negative\")\n        self._balance = balance\n\n    def balance(self):\n        return self._balance\n\nprint(Account(50).balance())",
  "Raising inside `__init__` stops the object from being created, so the caller gets the error instead of a broken object.",
  "Bank accounts, ages, prices and anything with rules. This idea is called **encapsulation**: the object guards its own data."),
C("Reject a bad port",
  "Valid ports run from 1 to 65535. Make FirewallRule raise `ValueError('port must be 1-65535')` for anything outside that range. The test at the bottom then prints: port must be 1-65535",
  "class FirewallRule:\n    def __init__(self, port):\n        # TODO: raise ValueError for a port outside 1-65535\n        self._port = port\n\ntry:\n    FirewallRule(70000)\n    print('No error')\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)",
  "class FirewallRule:\n    def __init__(self, port):\n        if not 1 <= port <= 65535:\n            raise ValueError('port must be 1-65535')\n        self._port = port\n\ntry:\n    FirewallRule(70000)\n    print('No error')\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)",
  "if not 1 <= port <= 65535:\n    raise ValueError('port must be 1-65535')",
  [("Check before storing", "Put the check at the top of `__init__`, before `self._port = port`. A chained comparison like `1 <= port <= 65535` tests both ends at once.",
    "n = 70\nprint(1 <= n <= 100, 1 <= 500 <= 100)  # True False"),
   ("The message is what gets printed", "`print(e)` shows the text you pass to ValueError, so it must match exactly: `'port must be 1-65535'`.",
    "try:\n    raise ValueError('oops')\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)  # oops")]),
Q("What does the underscore in `self._balance` tell other programmers?", ["It's internal: use the object's methods instead of touching it directly", "Python makes it read-only", "It's a private variable that can't be accessed"], "It's internal: use the object's methods instead of touching it directly",
  "It's only a naming convention. `acct._balance` still works, but it says \"this may change; don't rely on it\"."),
C("Methods that use other methods",
  "Add a `describe` method to Server that returns a status line using `self.uptime_hours()`. The print should show: web-01 up 26.0 h",
  "class Server:\n    def __init__(self, hostname, uptime_seconds):\n        self.hostname = hostname\n        self.uptime_seconds = uptime_seconds\n\n    def uptime_hours(self):\n        return self.uptime_seconds / 3600\n\n    # TODO: add describe(self) that returns 'HOSTNAME up N h'\n\nprint(Server('web-01', 93600).describe())",
  "class Server:\n    def __init__(self, hostname, uptime_seconds):\n        self.hostname = hostname\n        self.uptime_seconds = uptime_seconds\n\n    def uptime_hours(self):\n        return self.uptime_seconds / 3600\n\n    def describe(self):\n        return f'{self.hostname} up {self.uptime_hours()} h'\n\nprint(Server('web-01', 93600).describe())",
  "def describe(self):\n    return f'{self.hostname} up {self.uptime_hours()} h'",
  [("Call methods through self", "Inside a method, another method of the same object is `self.uptime_hours()`. Plain `uptime_hours()` raises NameError.",
    "class Pair:\n    def __init__(self, a, b):\n        self.a, self.b = a, b\n\n    def total(self):\n        return self.a + self.b\n\n    def report(self):\n        return f'total {self.total()}'\n\nprint(Pair(2, 3).report())"),
   ("Build the line", "An f-string can hold both values: `f'{self.hostname} up {self.uptime_hours()} h'`. Return it, since the last line prints what describe gives back.",
    "name = 'db-02'\nprint(f'{name} ok')")]),
]

INTERVIEW = [
Q("Interview question: what's the difference between a class and an object?",
  ["A class is the blueprint; an object is one instance built from it, with its own data", "They're two words for the same thing", "A class holds data and an object holds methods"],
  "A class is the blueprint; an object is one instance built from it, with its own data",
  "`Dog` describes what every dog has and does. `Dog('Rex')` and `Dog('Fido')` are two separate objects made from it."),
Q("What is `self` in a method?", ["The object the method was called on, passed in automatically", "A keyword that Python reserves for classes", "The class itself"],
  "The object the method was called on, passed in automatically",
  "`self` is just the conventional name for the first parameter. `obj.method()` fills it in with `obj`."),
Q("A class has `count = 0` in its body and `self.name = name` in `__init__`. Which is shared by every object?", ["`count`", "`name`", "Both", "Neither"], "`count`",
  "Variables in the class body are class attributes, shared by all objects. Attributes set on `self` belong to one object."),
Q("An object's attributes are stored in a dictionary (`obj.__dict__`). How long does reading `obj.name` take, as objects get more attributes?", ["O(1)", "O(n)", "O(log n)"], "O(1)",
  "Attribute lookup is a dictionary lookup, which takes about the same time however many attributes there are."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["TypeError", "hi", "Nothing"], "TypeError",
  "`hi` was defined without `self`, but `A().hi()` passes the object in anyway: \"hi() takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given\".",
  code="class A:\n    def hi():\n        print('hi')\n\ntry:\n    A().hi()\nexcept TypeError:\n    print('TypeError')",
  feedback={"hi": "Calling a method always passes the object as the first argument, and `hi` has no parameter to receive it."}),
C("Classic: build a stack",
  "Write a Stack class with `push(item)`, `pop()` (remove and return the top item), `peek()` (return the top without removing it) and `is_empty()`. The prints should show: 3, 3, 2, False",
  "class Stack:\n    # TODO: __init__, push, pop, peek, is_empty\n    pass\n\ns = Stack()\ns.push(1)\ns.push(2)\ns.push(3)\nprint(s.peek())\nprint(s.pop())\nprint(s.pop())\nprint(s.is_empty())",
  "class Stack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []\n\n    def push(self, item):\n        self.items.append(item)\n\n    def pop(self):\n        return self.items.pop()\n\n    def peek(self):\n        return self.items[-1]\n\n    def is_empty(self):\n        return len(self.items) == 0\n\ns = Stack()\ns.push(1)\ns.push(2)\ns.push(3)\nprint(s.peek())\nprint(s.pop())\nprint(s.pop())\nprint(s.is_empty())",
  "def __init__(self):\n    self.items = []\n\ndef push(self, item):\n    self.items.append(item)\n\ndef pop(self):\n    return self.items.pop()\n\ndef peek(self):\n    return self.items[-1]\n\ndef is_empty(self):\n    return len(self.items) == 0",
  [("A list does the work", "Keep a list in `self.items`. The end of the list is the top of the stack: `append` adds there and `pop()` removes from there, both O(1).",
    "items = []\nitems.append('a')\nitems.append('b')\nprint(items.pop(), items)  # b ['a']"),
   ("Peek reads the last item", "`self.items[-1]` is the top without removing it. `is_empty` returns whether the length is 0.",
    "items = [4, 5, 6]\nprint(items[-1], len(items) == 0)  # 6 False")]),
C("Classic: account lockout",
  "Write `login(password)` for UserAccount: return True for the right password (and reset the failure count), False for a wrong one. After 3 wrong passwords in a row, lock the account so every later login raises `PermissionError('account locked')`. It should print: False, False, True, then account locked",
  "class UserAccount:\n    def __init__(self, username, password):\n        self.username = username\n        self._password = password\n        self.failures = 0\n        self.locked = False\n\n    # TODO: login(self, password)\n\nacct = UserAccount('ada', 's3cret')\nprint(acct.login('guess1'))\nprint(acct.login('guess2'))\nprint(acct.login('s3cret'))\nfor guess in ['a', 'b', 'c']:\n    acct.login(guess)\ntry:\n    acct.login('s3cret')\nexcept PermissionError as e:\n    print(e)",
  "class UserAccount:\n    def __init__(self, username, password):\n        self.username = username\n        self._password = password\n        self.failures = 0\n        self.locked = False\n\n    def login(self, password):\n        if self.locked:\n            raise PermissionError('account locked')\n        if password == self._password:\n            self.failures = 0\n            return True\n        self.failures += 1\n        if self.failures >= 3:\n            self.locked = True\n        return False\n\nacct = UserAccount('ada', 's3cret')\nprint(acct.login('guess1'))\nprint(acct.login('guess2'))\nprint(acct.login('s3cret'))\nfor guess in ['a', 'b', 'c']:\n    acct.login(guess)\ntry:\n    acct.login('s3cret')\nexcept PermissionError as e:\n    print(e)",
  "def login(self, password):\n    if self.locked:\n        raise PermissionError('account locked')\n    if password == self._password:\n        self.failures = 0\n        return True\n    self.failures += 1\n    if self.failures >= 3:\n        self.locked = True\n    return False",
  [("Check the lock first", "A locked account must refuse even the right password, so test `self.locked` before comparing passwords. PermissionError is a built-in error, raised like ValueError.",
    "locked = True\ntry:\n    if locked:\n        raise PermissionError('no entry')\nexcept PermissionError as e:\n    print(e)"),
   ("Count consecutive failures", "A right password sets `self.failures = 0`. A wrong one adds 1, and at 3 it sets `self.locked = True`. That's why two misses, a success, then three misses locks the account.",
    "failures = 0\nfor ok in [False, False, True, False]:\n    failures = 0 if ok else failures + 1\nprint(failures)  # 1")]),
C("Classic: failed login tracker",
  "Write FailedLoginTracker with `record(ip)` to note a failed login from an address and `top()` to return the address with the most failures. It should print: 10.0.0.5",
  "class FailedLoginTracker:\n    # TODO: __init__, record, top\n    pass\n\nt = FailedLoginTracker()\nfor ip in ['10.0.0.5', '10.0.0.9', '10.0.0.5', '10.0.0.7', '10.0.0.5']:\n    t.record(ip)\nprint(t.top())",
  "class FailedLoginTracker:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.counts = {}\n\n    def record(self, ip):\n        self.counts[ip] = self.counts.get(ip, 0) + 1\n\n    def top(self):\n        return max(self.counts, key=self.counts.get)\n\nt = FailedLoginTracker()\nfor ip in ['10.0.0.5', '10.0.0.9', '10.0.0.5', '10.0.0.7', '10.0.0.5']:\n    t.record(ip)\nprint(t.top())",
  "def __init__(self):\n    self.counts = {}\n\ndef record(self, ip):\n    self.counts[ip] = self.counts.get(ip, 0) + 1\n\ndef top(self):\n    return max(self.counts, key=self.counts.get)",
  [("Store counts in a dictionary", "Create `self.counts = {}` in `__init__`. Each record adds 1 with `self.counts.get(ip, 0) + 1`.",
    "counts = {}\nfor w in ['a', 'b', 'a']:\n    counts[w] = counts.get(w, 0) + 1\nprint(counts)"),
   ("Find the biggest count", "`max(self.counts, key=self.counts.get)` returns the key with the largest value. Each record is O(1) and top is O(n) in the number of addresses: the core of a brute-force detector.",
    "scores = {'x': 2, 'y': 9}\nprint(max(scores, key=scores.get))  # y")]),
C("Debug: the missing hostname",
  "This crashes with AttributeError: Device has no attribute 'hostname'. `__init__` receives the hostname but never stores it on the object. Fix it so it prints: web-01",
  "class Device:\n    def __init__(self, hostname):\n        hostname = hostname\n\nd = Device('web-01')\nprint(d.hostname)",
  "class Device:\n    def __init__(self, hostname):\n        self.hostname = hostname\n\nd = Device('web-01')\nprint(d.hostname)",
  "Store it on self:\nself.hostname = hostname",
  [("A plain name is a local variable", "`hostname = hostname` assigns the parameter to itself, a local variable that disappears when `__init__` ends. The object gets nothing.",
    "def f(x):\n    x = x       # changes nothing outside\n    return 'done'\n\nprint(f(1))"),
   ("Attach it to the object", "Write `self.hostname = hostname`. The left side creates an attribute on this object; the right side is the parameter.",
    "class Tag:\n    def __init__(self, label):\n        self.label = label\n\nprint(Tag('new').label)")]),
]
